\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the \textit  {C.\ elegans}-matched ER topology. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. The same performance--invariance tradeoff appears without the biological topology. Across the sweep, sign placement, raw spectral radius, and the normalization factor also vary with the global sign fraction. A total of 50 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{15}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:er_frac_curves}{{8}{15}{Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the \textit {C.\ elegans}-matched ER topology. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. The same performance--invariance tradeoff appears without the biological topology. Across the sweep, sign placement, raw spectral radius, and the normalization factor also vary with the global sign fraction. A total of 50 trials were performed for each fraction}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the predicted-polarity-only \textit  {C.\ elegans} contact-count magnitude pattern after removing connections with unknown or complex signs. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. Varying the negative-edge fraction again traces the performance--invariance tradeoff with topology and unnormalized contact-count magnitudes held fixed; local sign placement, raw spectral radius, and the normalization factor vary across the sweep. A total of 200 trials were performed for each fraction.}}{15}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:og_cel_frac_curves}{{9}{15}{Mean performance and mean hyperparameter CV as a function of negative sign percentage for the predicted-polarity-only \textit {C.\ elegans} contact-count magnitude pattern after removing connections with unknown or complex signs. The truncated IPC diagnostic is denoted in orange, GR in red, MC in blue, and KR in green. Varying the negative-edge fraction again traces the performance--invariance tradeoff with topology and unnormalized contact-count magnitudes held fixed; local sign placement, raw spectral radius, and the normalization factor vary across the sweep. A total of 200 trials were performed for each fraction}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Mean raw spectral radius across sign-fraction sweeps for the ambiguous-edge \textit  {C.\ elegans} and \textit  {C.\ elegans}-matched ER topologies. The vertical dashed line marks the EleganSign-inferred negative-edge fraction, and the horizontal lines mark the corresponding unperturbed raw-radius baselines. In both topologies, the raw spectral radius decreases as the signs approach a balanced configuration. In the \textit  {C.\ elegans} sweep, it falls below the unperturbed baseline slightly before the inferred fraction is reached; this offset is consistent with a contribution from the simultaneous randomization of local sign placement. The ER-matched sweep shows a smaller offset, consistent with signs already being effectively random relative to topology. Each point represents the mean across 100 trials.}}{15}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sign_sweep_quicklook}{{10}{15}{Mean raw spectral radius across sign-fraction sweeps for the ambiguous-edge \textit {C.\ elegans} and \textit {C.\ elegans}-matched ER topologies. The vertical dashed line marks the EleganSign-inferred negative-edge fraction, and the horizontal lines mark the corresponding unperturbed raw-radius baselines. In both topologies, the raw spectral radius decreases as the signs approach a balanced configuration. In the \textit {C.\ elegans} sweep, it falls below the unperturbed baseline slightly before the inferred fraction is reached; this offset is consistent with a contribution from the simultaneous randomization of local sign placement. The ER-matched sweep shows a smaller offset, consistent with signs already being effectively random relative to topology. Each point represents the mean across 100 trials}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Reservoir activity under all-positive (0\% negative edges) and sign-balanced (50\% negative edges) versions of the ambiguous-edge \textit  {C.\ elegans} network. The topology and contact-count magnitudes are held fixed while edge signs are reassigned. Because each matrix is normalized separately to the same target radius, the comparison combines sign reassignment with different normalization factors determined by the two raw spectral radii. Panel A shows the total number of neuron--time samples for which the absolute reservoir state satisfies \( |x_i|\geq 0.5 \) during 1,000 post-washout time steps. Panel B shows the total number of transitions from \( |x_i|<0.5 \) to \( |x_i|\geq 0.5 \). Each dot is the mean across 50 repeats at one target spectral radius, \(\rho _{\mathrm  {target}}\in \{0.60,0.80,0.95,1.05\}\); bars show the mean of these four target-level means. The displayed raw spectral radii are means calculated before normalization.}}{16}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:activity_0_vs_50}{{11}{16}{Reservoir activity under all-positive (0\% negative edges) and sign-balanced (50\% negative edges) versions of the ambiguous-edge \textit {C.\ elegans} network. The topology and contact-count magnitudes are held fixed while edge signs are reassigned. Because each matrix is normalized separately to the same target radius, the comparison combines sign reassignment with different normalization factors determined by the two raw spectral radii. Panel A shows the total number of neuron--time samples for which the absolute reservoir state satisfies \( |x_i|\geq 0.5 \) during 1,000 post-washout time steps. Panel B shows the total number of transitions from \( |x_i|<0.5 \) to \( |x_i|\geq 0.5 \). Each dot is the mean across 50 repeats at one target spectral radius, \(\rho _{\mathrm {target}}\in \{0.60,0.80,0.95,1.05\}\); bars show the mean of these four target-level means. The displayed raw spectral radii are means calculated before normalization}{figure.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Mean absolute recurrent weight within closed three-neuron subgraphs across the negative-edge fraction sweep. A closed triad is defined here as a set of three neurons for which every unordered neuron pair is connected by at least one directed edge; each triad can therefore contain three to six directed edges. For each of the 6,527 closed triads, we calculate the mean absolute weight over its present directed edges after scaling the complete reservoir to the target spectral radius. Points show the mean of this triad-level statistic across 50 repeats, and error bars show the standard deviation across repeat-level means. Panels correspond to target spectral radii \(\rho _{\mathrm  {target}}=0.60\), \(0.80\), \(0.95\), and \(1.05\). Because topology and unnormalized weight magnitudes are held fixed, the variation across negative-edge fractions reflects changes in the global scaling required to reach each target spectral radius.}}{16}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:triad_sign_fraction}{{12}{16}{Mean absolute recurrent weight within closed three-neuron subgraphs across the negative-edge fraction sweep. A closed triad is defined here as a set of three neurons for which every unordered neuron pair is connected by at least one directed edge; each triad can therefore contain three to six directed edges. For each of the 6,527 closed triads, we calculate the mean absolute weight over its present directed edges after scaling the complete reservoir to the target spectral radius. Points show the mean of this triad-level statistic across 50 repeats, and error bars show the standard deviation across repeat-level means. Panels correspond to target spectral radii \(\rho _{\mathrm {target}}=0.60\), \(0.80\), \(0.95\), and \(1.05\). Because topology and unnormalized weight magnitudes are held fixed, the variation across negative-edge fractions reflects changes in the global scaling required to reach each target spectral radius}{figure.12}{}}
